 So, I start with the clustering problem and clustering is generally known as unsupervised

9
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:58,120
 learning and in the sense that we do not use any super supervised data or label data.

10
00:00:58,120 --> 00:01:04,260
 We have unlabeled data and more or less the broad objective of clustering is to figure

11
00:01:04,260 --> 00:01:10,640
 out the groups of objects which are similar to each other.

12
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:19,600
 So, while we do so we try to fulfill two objectives, one the objects and the items within a cluster

13
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:25,720
 should be similar that is one thing and then the objects and items from different clusters

14
00:01:25,720 --> 00:01:27,240
 should be dissimilar.

15
00:01:27,240 --> 00:01:34,400
 So, standing on these two objectives we will see several algorithms for clustering data

16
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:36,840
 points.

17
00:01:36,840 --> 00:01:43,180
 Now, there are many examples of clustering or application of clustering which you can

18
00:01:43,180 --> 00:01:44,180
 think of.

19
00:01:44,180 --> 00:01:50,280
 So, I have taken this particular example just to illustrate the utility of clustering algorithms.

20
00:01:50,280 --> 00:01:56,480
 For example, in shard result ranking, shard result clustering for instance when we see

21
00:01:56,480 --> 00:02:04,000
 some results in response to your query to a particular shard engine when we see a set

22
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:09,840
 of for example, set of documents, so inside the shard engine there are lot of things actually

23
00:02:09,840 --> 00:02:10,840
 going on.

24
00:02:10,840 --> 00:02:19,960
 For example, if your question is apples and we do not know whether this is the fruit apple

25
00:02:19,960 --> 00:02:24,520
 or this is the for example, the company apple and something like that we are never sure

26
00:02:24,520 --> 00:02:25,520
 about it.

27
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:33,600
 So, in a situation like that the best thing that a good shard engines can do is to group

28
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:40,480
 the web pages talking about the similar kind of content and then populate top few pages

29
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:45,520
 with various kinds of content covering wide range of things that possibly can cover all

30
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:48,320
 of the aspects that the query is trying to reflect.

31
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:54,440
 So, for example, few documents could be the apple company, it is talking about apple company,

32
00:02:54,440 --> 00:02:58,920
 few documents could be from for instance the apple fruit or something like that.

33
00:02:58,920 --> 00:03:04,200
 There are many such applications that one can think of where the clustering is really

34
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:05,200
 used.

35
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:13,480
 In fact, in many popular classification algorithms clustering is typically used as an intermediate

36
00:03:13,480 --> 00:03:19,560
 step to reduce some of the data annotation budget often.

37
00:03:19,560 --> 00:03:29,360
 So, let us take this particular example for instance, so in this case, for example, we

38
00:03:29,360 --> 00:03:35,080
 have this particular data, so for simplicity I have used two dimensional data, it has only

39
00:03:35,080 --> 00:03:38,280
 two features and so on so forth.

40
00:03:38,280 --> 00:03:46,160
 And so we have a data like this, so what we like to do in this case is to uncover the

41
00:03:46,160 --> 00:03:50,200
 underlying structure or rather the clusters of this data.

42
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:56,760
 So, apparently from this particular figure, what we can see is that that this particular

43
00:03:56,760 --> 00:04:04,760
 data is showing three clear clusters and so our objective would be to try to figure out

44
00:04:04,760 --> 00:04:07,560
 those clusters in an automatic way.

45
00:04:07,560 --> 00:04:13,320
 Now, note that we do not have any levels, that means we do not have any ground truth

46
00:04:13,320 --> 00:04:19,320
 for this one, we only have the feature vectors or some raw data or something like that and

47
00:04:19,320 --> 00:04:25,400
 we have to basically find out the groups automatically using some clustering technique.

48
00:04:25,400 --> 00:04:33,920
 So, what are the major issues in clustering and these are very important issues when you

49
00:04:33,920 --> 00:04:37,800
 are trying to perform any kind of clustering on any kind of data.

50
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:47,280
 So, the first issue is the representation for clustering, so you can have data with

51
00:04:47,280 --> 00:04:52,600
 explicit features, for example, in a tabular form data where the features are already known

52
00:04:52,600 --> 00:05:00,720
 to you, you can have a set of images, you can have a set of textual contents or documents,

53
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:04,640
 you can have videos and audios and so on so forth.

54
00:05:04,640 --> 00:05:11,720
 So data might come in various forms and therefore, before one is trying to apply any clustering

55
00:05:11,720 --> 00:05:17,840
 technique or algorithm, we need to understand how we can represent data, for example, converting

56
00:05:17,840 --> 00:05:24,960
 the raw data into a form that a particular clustering algorithm can understand, so which

57
00:05:24,960 --> 00:05:28,240
 is called feature engineering.

58
00:05:28,240 --> 00:05:33,240
 The second most important issue is basically the similarity or the distance between example.

59
00:05:33,240 --> 00:05:42,960
 So, all clustering algorithms will stand on similarity or dissimilarity measure and therefore,

60
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:47,720
 one has to define what kind of similarity or dissimilarity measure we are going to use

61
00:05:47,720 --> 00:05:54,360
 to group the objects because they are the backbone of any kind of clustering algorithm.

62
00:05:54,360 --> 00:06:00,560
 The second issue that we often encounter is that whether you should do flat clustering

63
00:06:00,560 --> 00:06:07,200
 or hierarchical clustering and we will spend some time on this particular aspect later

64
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:15,120
 in this particular lecture, so we will see how these things actually going to happen.

65
00:06:15,120 --> 00:06:20,360
 And the final thing is basically that how many clusters we would like to create.

66
00:06:20,360 --> 00:06:25,240
 Now whether we fix beforehand that we would like to create certain or 15 clusters from

67
00:06:25,240 --> 00:06:31,560
 the data or whether we can figure out from the data itself what would be the appropriate

68
00:06:31,560 --> 00:06:35,600
 number of clusters given the raw data.

69
00:06:35,600 --> 00:06:40,360
 So these are the three broad things, so the major things that one has to tackle when we

70
00:06:40,360 --> 00:06:47,280
 are trying to cluster the data points.

71
00:06:47,280 --> 00:06:54,960
 So there are broadly two types of clustering algorithms, one is flat algorithms.

72
00:06:54,960 --> 00:07:01,160
 So in this case we usually start with random partitions of the data and then what we do

73
00:07:01,160 --> 00:07:06,520
 we refine it iteratively for example, K-means algorithm which are going to see in little

74
00:07:06,520 --> 00:07:10,280
 more detail later on is one such example.

75
00:07:10,280 --> 00:07:17,480
 So in this case what we finally get is basically a disjoint set of groups.

76
00:07:17,480 --> 00:07:24,840
 That means each element or object will belong to exactly one group not more than one and

77
00:07:24,840 --> 00:07:30,280
 that is why you are calling flat clustering or partitional clustering often we call it.

78
00:07:30,280 --> 00:07:36,280
 Now the second category of algorithms which we call hierarchical algorithms we have brought

79
00:07:36,280 --> 00:07:42,320
 the two approaches in there bottom up or agglomerative or top down and divisive.

80
00:07:42,320 --> 00:07:49,240
 So in this case unlike the previous case in flat algorithms one particular element might

81
00:07:49,240 --> 00:07:56,560
 not belong to multiple clusters and in this case the clusters are typically organized hierarchically

82
00:07:56,560 --> 00:08:00,520
 sharing some sort of common relationship.

83
00:08:00,520 --> 00:08:07,680
 For example, hockey and cricket could be two different kinds of groups and again we can

84
00:08:07,680 --> 00:08:12,280
 group them together in a category called sports.

85
00:08:12,280 --> 00:08:16,600
 So hockey and cricket will be belonging to a lower level of category and then when you

86
00:08:16,600 --> 00:08:21,760
 mark them together to a bigger group we call sports that is basically you know one level

87
00:08:21,760 --> 00:08:23,760
 up in the hierarchy.

88
00:08:23,760 --> 00:08:28,400
 So also these are the kinds of things that we do often.

89
00:08:28,400 --> 00:08:34,800
 Now another categorization is basically the hard clustering and the soft clustering.

90
00:08:34,800 --> 00:08:42,760
 So in hard clustering each example or point belongs to exactly one cluster and in soft

91
00:08:42,760 --> 00:08:49,240
 clustering an example can belong to more than one cluster with a membership value.

92
00:08:49,240 --> 00:08:55,640
 In the sense that that it will tell whether a particular object or element is in a particular

93
00:08:55,640 --> 00:08:57,920
 cluster and to what extent.

94
00:08:57,920 --> 00:09:02,880
 So it is like a probabilistic membership for all of these points and it will tell you to

95
00:09:02,880 --> 00:09:10,040
 what extent a particular example is part of a particular cluster in your data something

96
00:09:10,040 --> 00:09:11,040
 like that we will do.

97
00:09:11,040 --> 00:09:17,140
 There are good reasons why we do that sometimes it is actually not possible in reality or

98
00:09:17,140 --> 00:09:22,240
 not very practical to assign a particular object to a single cluster we also would like to

99
00:09:22,240 --> 00:09:27,920
 know that what this possibility that this particular example is also a member of another

100
00:09:27,920 --> 00:09:28,920
 cluster.

101
00:09:28,920 --> 00:09:35,280
 So in a situation like that we basically try to apply soft clustering approaches.

102
00:09:35,280 --> 00:09:43,000
 Now we study our first flat clustering algorithm called K-means algorithm this is one of the

103
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:50,280
 oldest technique in machine learning and still very widely used for a simplicity, conceptual

104
00:09:50,280 --> 00:09:56,480
 simplicity, computational simplicity and also effectiveness.

105
00:09:56,480 --> 00:10:05,200
 So as I mentioned it is very efficient, simple and widely used because of a simplicity and

106
00:10:05,200 --> 00:10:09,520
 so the main steps of K-means are basically this.

107
00:10:09,520 --> 00:10:13,960
 First we start with some initial cluster centers.

108
00:10:13,960 --> 00:10:20,240
 So if we for example would like to create 10 cluster from data we start with say K-value

109
00:10:20,240 --> 00:10:21,240
 10.

110
00:10:21,240 --> 00:10:30,200
 So initially find out using some method that what would be the 10 initial cluster centers.

111
00:10:30,200 --> 00:10:37,020
 Then what we do we assign cluster for each member to the closest center which we call

112
00:10:37,020 --> 00:10:39,020
 assignment step.

113
00:10:39,020 --> 00:10:45,560
 So once we have the centers then for each element we take we figure out which center

114
00:10:45,560 --> 00:10:50,640
 is going to the closest to that particular cluster, particular point and then we assign

115
00:10:50,640 --> 00:10:53,880
 the point to that particular cluster.

116
00:10:53,880 --> 00:11:00,580
 And finally what we do we recalculate the centers as the mean of the points in the cluster

117
00:11:00,580 --> 00:11:02,080
 has changed.

118
00:11:02,080 --> 00:11:08,160
 So we start with a very weak guess which is often randomly done.

119
00:11:08,160 --> 00:11:14,840
 Then we do assignment step and then because we have done assignment steps the initial cluster

120
00:11:14,840 --> 00:11:16,960
 centers are not that valid.

121
00:11:16,960 --> 00:11:22,840
 So what we do is change it, refine it to find out better cluster centers and so on and so

122
00:11:22,840 --> 00:11:24,160
 on.

123
00:11:24,160 --> 00:11:28,920
 And we iterate step 2 and step 3.

124
00:11:28,920 --> 00:11:32,000
 We do not touch step 1, this is one time job.

125
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:37,040
 So we simply iterate step 2 and step 3 many many times to improve the quality of centers

126
00:11:37,040 --> 00:11:43,240
 and correspondingly quality of clusters of the data.

127
00:11:43,240 --> 00:11:45,760
 So we need to stop.

128
00:11:45,760 --> 00:11:49,800
 So typically we stop using one of these two possibilities.

129
00:11:49,800 --> 00:11:56,880
 One we stop after number fixed iteration that is pretty common often we do or we see that

130
00:11:56,880 --> 00:12:03,760
 in two successive iterations if the centers or the memberships are not changing then we

131
00:12:03,760 --> 00:12:07,520
 can also stop.

132
00:12:07,520 --> 00:12:14,120
 So we will see how K-Means actually works with this small example.

133
00:12:14,120 --> 00:12:21,400
 We assume that we have these data points which we would like to cluster.

134
00:12:21,400 --> 00:12:25,840
 So we say we would like to create three clusters from this data.

135
00:12:25,840 --> 00:12:30,840
 So in K-Means you have to specify the number of clusters that you want to create the value

136
00:12:30,840 --> 00:12:37,280
 of K because that is a precondition that you have to follow.

137
00:12:37,280 --> 00:12:42,000
 So therefore we are going to create three clusters from this data.

138
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:52,240
 So our first step will require that we initialize three centroids and typically we do it randomly

139
00:12:52,240 --> 00:12:53,880
 from the original data.

140
00:12:53,880 --> 00:12:58,400
 So now say that we have chosen three random points with three different colored boxes

141
00:12:58,400 --> 00:13:03,200
 you can say blue, red and pink and so on so forth.

142
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:10,640
 So these are three initial centers that we have chosen randomly from the original data.

143
00:13:10,640 --> 00:13:18,440
 Now once we have done that then in step two our objective would be to assign cluster memberships

144
00:13:18,440 --> 00:13:21,800
 for each of these data points in my data.

145
00:13:21,800 --> 00:13:27,120
 So who will be the blue one, who will be the red one and who will be the pink one.

146
00:13:27,120 --> 00:13:31,320
 So that is what we have to basically decide in this case.

147
00:13:31,320 --> 00:13:39,180
 So we do that by computing the closest cluster center that we have right now.

148
00:13:39,180 --> 00:13:46,560
 So if we go by that rule, what happens is something like this that we have at this assignment.

149
00:13:46,560 --> 00:13:53,800
 So the color codes basically tell us that which element is going to be in which cluster.

150
00:13:53,800 --> 00:13:59,400
 So the blue ones are closest to the blue square initial centers, the red ones are the red

151
00:13:59,400 --> 00:14:05,120
 one and the pinks basically for the pink square initial centers.

152
00:14:05,120 --> 00:14:13,120
 Now once we have done this step, the earlier cluster centers that we have done randomly

153
00:14:13,120 --> 00:14:19,560
 is no longer valid because you have assigned the cluster memberships and basically we need

154
00:14:19,560 --> 00:14:26,200
 to update the cluster centers from what we had in the previous step.

155
00:14:26,200 --> 00:14:31,840
 So what we do basically we take some kind of mean of all these elements somewhere in

156
00:14:31,840 --> 00:14:36,000
 the middle, the new centers will be lying.

157
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:41,520
 So if we do that which is basically step 3, then we are going to get something like this.

158
00:14:41,520 --> 00:14:47,680
 Now we can see the squares are basically the centroids for each of these clusters in this

159
00:14:47,680 --> 00:14:50,880
 case.

160
00:14:50,880 --> 00:14:56,280
 So that is the first completion of the first iteration of our algorithm.

161
00:14:56,280 --> 00:15:02,160
 So now that we have slightly better centroids compared to the random one that we had.

162
00:15:02,160 --> 00:15:09,600
 Now we have to iterate step 2 and step 3 repeatedly in order to improve our quality of centroids

163
00:15:09,600 --> 00:15:13,400
 and consequently the quality of clusters.

164
00:15:13,400 --> 00:15:19,840
 So again in step 2 what we do, we basically again now that we have new centroids that

165
00:15:19,840 --> 00:15:26,960
 we again try to compute the cluster memberships for each of these data points, so if we do

166
00:15:26,960 --> 00:15:31,040
 that we are going to get something like this.

167
00:15:31,040 --> 00:15:37,320
 Some members will change their cluster memberships for sure because you now have different sets

168
00:15:37,320 --> 00:15:41,680
 of centroids after updating the original one.

169
00:15:41,680 --> 00:15:46,120
 So that is the basically the step 2 in the next iteration.

170
00:15:46,120 --> 00:15:50,480
 So now again since because we have new memberships and so on so forth, so what we do again we

171
00:15:50,480 --> 00:15:57,200
 have to apply step 3 which is basically readjusting or recomputing the centroids is the mean thing.

172
00:15:57,200 --> 00:16:01,600
 So which we do and we are going to get something like this again.

173
00:16:01,600 --> 00:16:06,400
 So this is the completion of iteration 2, sorry there is a slight typographical mistake

174
00:16:06,400 --> 00:16:10,960
 it should be iteration 2 completion not iteration 1.

175
00:16:10,960 --> 00:16:16,440
 So therefore essentially what we have to do we have to basically iterate step 1, step 3

176
00:16:16,440 --> 00:16:21,840
 multiple times to improve our cluster quality.

177
00:16:21,840 --> 00:16:26,560
 So again we do the same thing we simply find out the cluster memberships for each of the

178
00:16:26,560 --> 00:16:32,560
 data points which is step 2 then again readjust the cluster centroids and so on and so forth.

179
00:16:32,560 --> 00:16:37,200
 So this is the completion of iteration 3.

180
00:16:37,200 --> 00:16:43,560
 So in this way we simply move on until and unless either we basically have reached the

181
00:16:43,560 --> 00:16:51,400
 required number of iterations that we set or we see that the centroids are not changing.

182
00:16:51,400 --> 00:16:57,040
 So when the centroids are not changing nothing you can gain by doing lot of iterations.

183
00:16:57,040 --> 00:17:03,880
 So we know that we are more or less rich to stable solution and so we will stop.

184
00:17:03,880 --> 00:17:08,920
 So there is no change so basically we will stop in this case.

185
00:17:08,920 --> 00:17:16,420
 So now the question is there are two important questions that we have to address one that

186
00:17:16,420 --> 00:17:21,600
 how to assign the cluster in each example to the cluster center and then how to recalculate

187
00:17:21,600 --> 00:17:23,600
 the centers.

188
00:17:23,600 --> 00:17:27,400
 So you know we have to figure out which one is going to be the closest to which particular

189
00:17:27,400 --> 00:17:31,280
 cluster centroid and so on and so forth.

190
00:17:31,280 --> 00:17:36,940
 So we do this by using a distance or similarity measure.

191
00:17:36,940 --> 00:17:41,440
 So typically we use distance measure one can also use similarity measure no problem.

192
00:17:41,440 --> 00:17:49,720
 So what we do basically for each data point we find out the distance between all the centroids

193
00:17:49,720 --> 00:17:56,480
 in my data in this case we can see using three different colors because we had three centroids

194
00:17:56,480 --> 00:18:03,560
 and the blue one basically the circle blue circle is basically the one which we are considering

195
00:18:03,560 --> 00:18:07,080
 okay to try to assign the membership of.

196
00:18:07,080 --> 00:18:11,720
 So we figured out the distance between this particular point and three cluster centroids

197
00:18:11,720 --> 00:18:18,320
 okay and then like assign this to a centroid which like where you have the minimum distance

198
00:18:18,320 --> 00:18:21,680
 okay.

199
00:18:21,680 --> 00:18:26,880
 So there are a lot of distance measure that people who can choose or similarity measures

200
00:18:26,880 --> 00:18:28,720
 people can choose.

201
00:18:28,720 --> 00:18:33,440
 So one of the very common distance measure that we use is called Euclidean distance.

202
00:18:33,440 --> 00:18:40,680
 So of course objective given two vectors say n dimensional vectors okay of two points we

203
00:18:40,680 --> 00:18:44,600
 simply want to compute the distance between these two points which is like nothing but

204
00:18:44,600 --> 00:18:49,400
 a numerical value a number okay telling one number telling how far these two points are

205
00:18:49,400 --> 00:18:50,400
 okay.

206
00:18:50,400 --> 00:18:55,520
 So you can use because centroid is a vector and a point is also a vector on the same dimension

207
00:18:55,520 --> 00:18:59,040
 so you can easily compute this number and so on so forth.

208
00:18:59,040 --> 00:19:03,040
 Now I am just taking one example of distance measure there are other distance measures

209
00:19:03,040 --> 00:19:08,360
 like Minkowski's distance then you have Manhattan distance then you have Molonov's distance

210
00:19:08,360 --> 00:19:09,360
 and so on so forth.

211
00:19:09,360 --> 00:19:13,600
 There are a lot of distance measures so depending upon the particular application of the nature

212
00:19:13,600 --> 00:19:16,880
 of the data one can use any of this distance measure.

213
00:19:16,880 --> 00:19:23,800
 So K means is a general purpose algorithm and it is not the case that Euclidean has

214
00:19:23,800 --> 00:19:27,560
 to be used one can use any other things.

215
00:19:27,560 --> 00:19:32,600
 So next is basically the recalculating the centers.

216
00:19:32,600 --> 00:19:37,200
 Now once you change the cluster membership and so on so forth we have to recalculate

217
00:19:37,200 --> 00:19:40,760
 the centers using a certain set of points.

218
00:19:40,760 --> 00:19:47,920
 So for example in this case we would like to figure out what would be the central point

219
00:19:47,920 --> 00:19:54,960
 for the blue cluster, central point for the red cluster and central point from the purple

220
00:19:54,960 --> 00:19:55,960
 the pink cluster.

221
00:19:55,960 --> 00:19:59,600
 So these are the three things basically have to figure out because centroid is something

222
00:19:59,600 --> 00:20:06,520
 that we will be using to figure out the cluster membership in every iteration.

223
00:20:06,520 --> 00:20:15,120
 So essentially we have to figure out these square boxes out of this like this blue, red

224
00:20:15,120 --> 00:20:16,640
 and pink data.

225
00:20:16,640 --> 00:20:20,200
 So how do we compute that number?

226
00:20:20,200 --> 00:20:25,760
 So here is a simple thing essentially you take a set of vectors from a particular cluster

227
00:20:25,760 --> 00:20:30,680
 so we are interested in figuring out the centroid for the blue one.

228
00:20:30,680 --> 00:20:36,560
 So what we do basically we simply take all the blue points and take the min vector of

229
00:20:36,560 --> 00:20:37,840
 that one.

230
00:20:37,840 --> 00:20:43,600
 So min vector is nothing but simply we add all these vectors and simply divide by the

231
00:20:43,600 --> 00:20:46,320
 number of data points that you have in a particular cluster.

232
00:20:46,320 --> 00:20:53,280
 It is a simple addition thing that you can do.

233
00:20:53,280 --> 00:20:59,960
 Now these are so far it is a mechanism for doing K-means clustering.

234
00:20:59,960 --> 00:21:05,440
 Now actually what are you trying to achieve in this case?

235
00:21:05,440 --> 00:21:11,080
 Now because you already have seen quite a bit of machine learning models in this particular

236
00:21:11,080 --> 00:21:13,520
 series of lectures.

237
00:21:13,520 --> 00:21:19,600
 So one of the key things in any machine learning model is an objective function or loss function

238
00:21:19,600 --> 00:21:23,520
 or profit function or whatever you can call it.

239
00:21:23,520 --> 00:21:27,480
 We often call it objective function.

240
00:21:27,480 --> 00:21:31,280
 So either you maximize it or minimize it something like we have to do.

241
00:21:31,280 --> 00:21:38,000
 So in this case also K-means is not like just a plain simple algorithm it is also trying

242
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:42,840
 to minimize a thing called K-means loss.

243
00:21:42,840 --> 00:21:51,020
 So K-means loss is nothing but so value which will indicate the quality of clusters that

244
00:21:51,020 --> 00:21:53,200
 you have generated.

245
00:21:53,200 --> 00:22:01,880
 So the expression in this case what you can see is something like this that we take every

246
00:22:01,880 --> 00:22:06,680
 data point in this case this is Xi.

247
00:22:06,680 --> 00:22:15,440
 We take the cluster center closest to the data point Xi which is like basically the

248
00:22:15,440 --> 00:22:20,800
 mu K in this case and we find out the distance between the data point and the closest cluster

249
00:22:20,800 --> 00:22:25,900
 center and simply add them up for all the data points.

250
00:22:25,900 --> 00:22:33,520
 So essentially what this is telling you is basically that to what extent the data points

251
00:22:33,520 --> 00:22:42,160
 are to what extent the data points are dissimilar to or similar to the cluster centers in which

252
00:22:42,160 --> 00:22:43,160
 it belongs.

253
00:22:43,160 --> 00:22:49,440
 So if the loss value is very high then naturally you know the data points which are belonging

254
00:22:49,440 --> 00:22:54,360
 to a particular cluster are far apart from the centroids in this case you can expect

255
00:22:54,360 --> 00:22:56,960
 that the clusters are basically dispersed.

256
00:22:56,960 --> 00:23:02,240
 If it is small then you know the clusters are actually very compact so that is the physical

257
00:23:02,240 --> 00:23:06,960
 intuition or the explanation behind this particular loss function.

258
00:23:06,960 --> 00:23:14,280
 So K-means function the K-means algorithm that we have seen so far it attempts to minimize

259
00:23:14,280 --> 00:23:21,320
 this loss value as much as possible but of course like all difficult problems, clustering

260
00:23:21,320 --> 00:23:25,840
 problems is also controversial very challenging so there is no guarantee that you are going

261
00:23:25,840 --> 00:23:32,360
 to get the optimal loss value in this case but you can get somewhat good value depending

262
00:23:32,360 --> 00:23:39,680
 upon how you are starting up with and so on and so forth.

263
00:23:39,680 --> 00:23:47,360
 Now I started up with by saying that K-means is a very fast algorithm very efficient algorithm

264
00:23:47,360 --> 00:23:52,600
 and so we like to see little bit of mathematical analysis of the computational budget for the

265
00:23:52,600 --> 00:23:55,760
 K-means algorithm.

266
00:23:55,760 --> 00:24:00,320
 So in order to understand that we try to understand the computational overhead in every step of

267
00:24:00,320 --> 00:24:02,760
 this iteration.

268
00:24:02,760 --> 00:24:09,640
 So one step is basically the assignment step where we are trying to figure out the closest

269
00:24:09,640 --> 00:24:13,600
 cluster center for a particular point.

270
00:24:13,600 --> 00:24:22,000
 So if you have K centers and N data points then a single pass for every data point we

271
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:26,920
 have to compute the distance between all the K centers so in that case the complexity is

272
00:24:26,920 --> 00:24:35,520
 going to be order of KN and then in the next step we will have to compute, recalculate the

273
00:24:35,520 --> 00:24:39,280
 cluster center which is nothing but a linear term algorithm.

274
00:24:39,280 --> 00:24:43,440
 So this is a computational budget for one particular iteration.

275
00:24:43,440 --> 00:24:49,760
 Now if you repeat these steps many many times we often do for example say P times or so

276
00:24:49,760 --> 00:24:55,200
 on and so forth then you simply multiply P into with this particular numbers.

277
00:24:55,200 --> 00:24:59,920
 So that is basically the complexity of this K-means algorithm.

278
00:24:59,920 --> 00:25:04,280
 But to give you sort of the final verdict on this one K-means is generally a linear time

279
00:25:04,280 --> 00:25:12,200
 algorithm assuming that the dimension of this data is basically not so high so under the

280
00:25:12,200 --> 00:25:21,200
 assumption it is typically a linear time algorithm and therefore it is extremely fast.

281
00:25:21,200 --> 00:25:30,720
 Now there are few important issues, practical issues rather which we have to deal with.

282
00:25:30,720 --> 00:25:35,640
 So fast issue is basically the number of clusters that we would like to create.

283
00:25:35,640 --> 00:25:40,000
 So you have to tell K-means algorithm that this many clusters I would like to create.

284
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:43,040
 So how to do that?

285
00:25:43,040 --> 00:25:51,800
 Second issue the convergence issue that means when we are going to stop this algorithm and

286
00:25:51,800 --> 00:25:57,600
 the third issue perhaps one of the most important issue is basically the initial cluster center

287
00:25:57,600 --> 00:26:06,560
 selection, how we are going to select the initial cluster centers so that we do not

288
00:26:06,560 --> 00:26:10,480
 create bad quality clusters.

289
00:26:10,480 --> 00:26:15,280
 So these three things we need to now address and we will see one by one how we can address

290
00:26:15,280 --> 00:26:19,960
 these three important things.

291
00:26:19,960 --> 00:26:25,160
 So first let us look at how we choose the value of K or at least an attempt to choose

292
00:26:25,160 --> 00:26:29,400
 value of K in a systematic way because we never know from our original data that how

293
00:26:29,400 --> 00:26:33,880
 many clusters are there and if the data is high dimensional this is even more difficult

294
00:26:33,880 --> 00:26:37,400
 to figure out visually that this many clusters are there.

295
00:26:37,400 --> 00:26:44,280
 So therefore we need to have some systematic way of figuring out the value of K.

296
00:26:44,280 --> 00:26:50,520
 So one popular approach that people follow is called elbow method.

297
00:26:50,520 --> 00:26:56,800
 So in this particular method what we do is something like this.

298
00:26:56,800 --> 00:27:03,880
 So we have a data then we run K-means with different K-values starting from 2 for example

299
00:27:03,880 --> 00:27:08,240
 to a very high value say 100.

300
00:27:08,240 --> 00:27:15,840
 So for every K-value you run the K-means algorithm and it will produce the corresponding K-means

301
00:27:15,840 --> 00:27:18,520
 loss which we discussed earlier.

302
00:27:18,520 --> 00:27:26,760
 So loss is going to tell you to some extent the quality of clusters that you have generated.

303
00:27:26,760 --> 00:27:32,320
 So you simply plot a graph like this the x axis is the K in the number of clusters that

304
00:27:32,320 --> 00:27:37,600
 you produced and y axis is the loss value that you also produced from this particular

305
00:27:37,600 --> 00:27:39,800
 clustering.

306
00:27:39,800 --> 00:27:48,480
 So now what we do we basically plot this graph and we try to look at if there is any clear

307
00:27:48,480 --> 00:27:52,040
 pattern in this particular graph.

308
00:27:52,040 --> 00:28:00,560
 So if you see that the graph is showing a elbow like structure a bending at elbow that

309
00:28:00,560 --> 00:28:07,080
 means it is basically you know dropping at a very high rate and then suddenly it gets

310
00:28:07,080 --> 00:28:11,200
 flattened okay it becomes very flat.

311
00:28:11,200 --> 00:28:19,600
 So if you have observed such a pattern like this then possibly the point where you see

312
00:28:19,600 --> 00:28:28,840
 that the value dropped very abruptly and then flattened so this is the point to be chosen

313
00:28:28,840 --> 00:28:29,840
 as a number of clusters.

314
00:28:29,840 --> 00:28:35,080
 So in this case for example the good value could be 3 in this case.

315
00:28:35,080 --> 00:28:44,760
 Now while I am saying this this is a very well known technique and therefore I must

316
00:28:44,760 --> 00:28:51,840
 give you a small warning is that that this is sort of a guideline not necessarily a full

317
00:28:51,840 --> 00:28:56,960
 group method okay it does not guarantee that if you apply this technique you are going

318
00:28:56,960 --> 00:29:02,520
 to find out the right number of clusters okay so this is not the case always.

319
00:29:02,520 --> 00:29:07,820
 It can give you some kind of guideline but it does not guarantee that this particular

320
00:29:07,820 --> 00:29:10,560
 key value is going to be the optimal one okay.

321
00:29:10,560 --> 00:29:16,560
 So therefore this has to be used very very carefully.

322
00:29:16,560 --> 00:29:23,960
 The second approach that we use is called shield weight measure.

323
00:29:23,960 --> 00:29:28,320
 So the key idea for good clustering which I mentioned at the very beginning is this

324
00:29:28,320 --> 00:29:37,360
 that the value has to be small within cluster variance has to be small okay that means the

325
00:29:37,360 --> 00:29:44,400
 points which are belonging to a particular cluster it is going to be very small and then

326
00:29:44,400 --> 00:29:51,080
 the between cluster variance has to be very large because that is basically a two broad

327
00:29:51,080 --> 00:29:57,920
 criteria that we try to fulfill in any clustering algorithm.

328
00:29:57,920 --> 00:30:04,600
 So we will be standing on these two criteria to use another measure to try to figure out

329
00:30:04,600 --> 00:30:09,160
 a reasonably good value of k.

330
00:30:09,160 --> 00:30:17,360
 So in this particular case we use within cluster measure which is nothing but a measure which

331
00:30:17,360 --> 00:30:22,160
 tells you to what extent the clusters are compact.

332
00:30:22,160 --> 00:30:29,400
 So if you can look at this particular expression the C i is the cluster for data point i, D

333
00:30:29,400 --> 00:30:31,800
 ij is nothing but the distance between i and j.

334
00:30:31,800 --> 00:30:36,600
 So you take all pairs of data points in a particular cluster okay and then compute their

335
00:30:36,600 --> 00:30:40,720
 distances you take some sort of average distance between them and so on so forth okay.

336
00:30:40,720 --> 00:30:46,720
 So essentially this is telling intuitively that what is the average pair wise distance

337
00:30:46,720 --> 00:30:51,160
 of points those who are belonging to a particular cluster okay.

338
00:30:51,160 --> 00:30:57,900
 So this will to some extent reflect the compactness or closeness of a particular cluster.

339
00:30:57,900 --> 00:31:02,000
 Now the next important criterion is basically between cluster variance.

340
00:31:02,000 --> 00:31:07,920
 So in this case we take data points from different clusters from the same cluster.

341
00:31:07,920 --> 00:31:12,600
 So if you look at this in this case is basically we take data points from different clusters

342
00:31:12,600 --> 00:31:17,720
 and compute the distance between the different points and so on so forth.

343
00:31:17,720 --> 00:31:23,420
 And then we use this to quantities to compute the silhouettes measure which is nothing but

344
00:31:23,420 --> 00:31:30,600
 a measure telling to what extent the cluster quality is good or bad.

345
00:31:30,600 --> 00:31:39,640
 Now so this measure again once again will be used to kind of plot a graph and then try

346
00:31:39,640 --> 00:31:44,480
 to figure out what would be the good reasonably good value of K okay.

347
00:31:44,480 --> 00:31:49,440
 So in this case unlike the K means loss value higher score is actually better.

348
00:31:49,440 --> 00:31:54,120
 So if you get higher score for a particular K value for silhouettes measure that means

349
00:31:54,120 --> 00:31:56,440
 the cluster quality is good.

350
00:31:56,440 --> 00:32:04,360
 So once again similar to the previous approach what we do we run this algorithm K means algorithm

351
00:32:04,360 --> 00:32:09,200
 for different K many many times started from 2 to say for example 100 or 200 depending

352
00:32:09,200 --> 00:32:18,720
 upon your data and then again we plot the K versus the silver score x axis is K y axis

353
00:32:18,720 --> 00:32:25,520
 is the silver score and we look at the graph and we see a pattern something like this.

354
00:32:25,520 --> 00:32:33,240
 So what you can see in this case is that the value initially was very small slowly increasing

355
00:32:33,240 --> 00:32:40,600
 then it started decreasing suddenly okay so you can think about something like this basically

356
00:32:40,600 --> 00:32:46,560
 the angle that you can see okay smaller the angle sharper the changes and so on so forth.

357
00:32:46,560 --> 00:32:50,760
 So the moment you see something like that increasing and then suddenly started decreasing

358
00:32:50,760 --> 00:32:56,400
 then this is the point which can reflect that the number of clusters is good one.

359
00:32:56,400 --> 00:33:01,640
 So in this case also the value could be 3 is a good number of clusters once again just

360
00:33:01,640 --> 00:33:08,340
 like the previous case this is not a guarantee that this value will actually reflect what

361
00:33:08,340 --> 00:33:13,720
 is the optimal number of clusters that is often a tricky thing we never know optimal

362
00:33:13,720 --> 00:33:18,000
 number of clusters before and because this is an unsupervised learning things become

363
00:33:18,000 --> 00:33:22,200
 even more difficult because we are dealing with high dimensional data so you can never

364
00:33:22,200 --> 00:33:27,560
 plot this one on a real like a figure which a human can understand.

365
00:33:27,560 --> 00:33:33,080
 So while you can use these things as a guideline to figure out what would be the possible good

366
00:33:33,080 --> 00:33:39,800
 values of K it has to be used also the other factors like the quality of results that you

367
00:33:39,800 --> 00:33:47,720
 are finally getting by using this clustering techniques and so on so forth.

368
00:33:47,720 --> 00:33:54,160
 Now the another important issue is basically the initial cluster center or we often call

369
00:33:54,160 --> 00:34:01,520
 it seed selection so remember that the first step of K-minus clustering algorithm is basically

370
00:34:01,520 --> 00:34:10,400
 the seed selection approach so in seed selection what we try to do we try to figure out what

371
00:34:10,400 --> 00:34:18,400
 would be the good number of centers sorry good centers that we will use to start with.

372
00:34:18,400 --> 00:34:25,320
 So if the centers are bad then you know we are going to get very bad quality clusters

373
00:34:25,320 --> 00:34:34,920
 so we will see strategies of figuring out the goods androids in this particular case.

374
00:34:34,920 --> 00:34:44,880
 So we see in this case that we initially like chose the centers randomly okay so now let

375
00:34:44,880 --> 00:34:52,600
 us see what happens the consequences of random center selection okay now say my original

376
00:34:52,600 --> 00:34:59,960
 data from my original data I selected the centers randomly so if I do that what will

377
00:34:59,960 --> 00:35:09,280
 happen so this is going to get something like this once you find out the cluster memberships

